data: {"id":"chatcmpl-r2q1m5oqd4ltj0hm8u0be","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1789817556,"model":"gemma-4-12b-it-qat","system_fingerprint":"gemma-4-12b-it-qat","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"tool_calls":[{"index":0,"type":"function","function":{"arguments":"{\"content\":\"def solve(n):\\n    # The problem description is quite cryptic. \\n    # \\\"Write a positive whole number the way it is said when counting positions: \\n    # the number itself followed by the two-letter ending that goes with it.\\\"\\n    # \\\"The three teens that sit between ten and fourteen are the well-known exception \\n    # and take the plain ending. The answer is text.\\\"\\n\\n    # Let's re-examine: \\\"the number itself\\\" + \\\"two-letter ending\\\".\\n    # If n=1, maybe it's \\\"one\\\" + \\\"st\\\"? No, that's not how it's said.\\n    # Wait! What if the \\\"number itself\\\" is NOT the word? \\n    # What if it's just the digits? Like \\\"1st\\\", \\\"2nd\\\"? But it says \\\"The answer is text.\\\"\\n    # Maybe it means \\\"first\\\", \\\"second\\\", \\\"third\\\"... but those are not \\\"the number itself followed by a two-letter ending\\\".\\n\\n    # Let's think about the words for numbers: one, two, three, four, five, six, seven, eight, nine, ten...\\n    # And their ordinal forms: first, second, third, fourth, fifth, sixth, seventh, eighth, ninth, tenth...\\n    # None of these are \\\"number + 2 letters\\\".\\n\\n    # Wait! What if the \\\"two-letter ending\\\" is something like \\\"st\\\", \\\"nd\\\", \\\"rd\\\"?\\n    # But those are not all two letters. 'th' is two letters.\\n    # If n=1, it's \\\"first\\\".\\n    # If n=2, it's \\\"second\\\".\\n    # If n=3, it's \\\"third\\\".\\n    # If n=4, it's \\\"fourth\\\".\\n    # ...\\n    # If n=10, it's \\\"tenth\\\".\\n    # If n=11, it's \\\"eleventh\\\".\\n    # If n=12, it's \\\"twelfth\\\".\\n    # If n=13, it's \\\"thirteenth\\\".\\n    # If n=14, it's \\\"fourteenth\\\".\\n\\n    # The prompt says 11, 12, 13 are exceptions and take the \\\"plain ending\\\".\\n    # What is a \\\"plain ending\\\"? Maybe they don't have an ending?\\n    # So 11 -> eleven, 12 -> twelve, 13 -> thirteen.\\n    # And for others, we add an ending?\\n    # But what ending? \\\"the two-letter ending that goes with it\\\".\\n    # For most numbers, the ordinal suffix is 'th'. That's two letters!\\n    # So if n=1, maybe it's \\\"oneth\\\"? No, that's not how it's said.\\n\\n    # Let me try to think of another way to say positions.\\n    # 1st = first\\n    # 2nd = second\\n    # 3rd = third\\n    # 4th = fourth\\n    # ...\\n    # Maybe the \\\"number itself\\\" is the word for the number?\\n    # one, two, three, four...\\n    # And the ending is \\\"st\\\", \\\"nd\\\", \\\"rd\\\"? But those are not all two letters.\\n\\n    # Wait! I just realized something. \\n    # What if it's about the words:\\n    # 1st -> first\\n    # 2nd -> second\\n    # 3rd -> third\\n    # 4th -> fourth\\n    # ...\\n    # No, that doesn't fit \\\"number itself followed by two-letter ending\\\".\\n\\n    # Let me try to search for the phrase again. I will use a different approach.\\n    pass\\n\",\"path\":\"solution.py\"}"}}]},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":null}]}

